> Implementing a custom backend
__doc_meta__: true
description: 'Creating a custom backend'
<

Creating a custom backend for configatron allows you full control over config
loading. The process is simple: you need to implement a subclass of the
``configatron.backends.CfgBackend`` protocol:

>
__embed__: 'code/python'
    from typing import Any

    from configatron.backends import CfgBackend
    from configatron.backends import KeyspaceSummary
    from configatron.types import CfgFieldDesc


    class SecretsManagerBackend(CfgBackend):
        """A custom config backend that contacts a cloud provider's secrets
        manager.
        """
        ALLOW_SECRET = True
        ALLOW_PLAINTEXT = False

        def load_sync(
                self,
                request: KeyspaceSummary,
                full_keyspace: KeyspaceSummary
                ) -> dict[CfgFieldDesc, Any]:
            """Required to support sync loading, optional for async-only.
            """
            raise NotImplementedError('Your implementation goes here!')

        async def load_async(
                self,
                request: KeyspaceSummary,
                full_keyspace: KeyspaceSummary
                ) -> dict[CfgFieldDesc, Any]:
            """Required to support async loading, optional for sync-only.
            """
            raise NotImplementedError('Your implementation goes here!')

Note that ``request`` indicates the config fields currently being requested,
and ``full_keyspace`` indicates all possible config fields for that backend.
During initial config loading, these values will be the same; however, if and
when ``configatron`` implements dynamic config polling, the full keyspace is
included so that backends can preemptively update non-expired keys, if doing so
would be effectively free anyways.

For example, if you're loading all of your config values from a json file, then
you're going to be downloading the whole json file regardless. So if the backend
gets a request to update a single config field from the file, it might as well
return any other fields from that value at the same time.

Note that you should set the values of ``ALLOW_SECRET`` and ``ALLOW_PLAINTEXT``
according to your actual needs. Backends that don't support secrets will never
be asked to load a field declared as secret (ie, it will never appear in the
``request``, nor the ``full_keyspace``). If the backend nonetheless includes
a value declared as secret, it will be ignored by the config manager during
loading.
